1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,660
In this lesson, we'll go through what a knowledge graph means and how they help represent and retrieve relationships from your data.

2
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:10,780
You'll then explore the dataset you'll work on to build a knowledge graph.

3
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:12,020
Let's dive in.

4
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:14,040
So, what is a knowledge graph?

5
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:21,520
We're going to take a step back for a moment and reflect on relational databases and what a relational schema looks like.

6
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:28,100
We all probably are familiar with some scenario like this, where there'll be some table on the left and some table on the right,

7
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:35,760
and this table in the middle, that is the join table, that allows for multiple connections between the persons and the products in this particular example.

8
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:38,680
So, let's think about these two tables with the join table.

9
00:00:39,460 --> 00:00:42,960
If you ask a question like, what products has this person purchased?

10
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:48,380
You'd first start with the person table and select this person here, it's identified by ABK.

11
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:51,980
And, of course, you do a join over to the person product table,

12
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:57,320
and you'd see that the person product table has a join between the ABK and some share,

13
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,560
ABK and a lamp, ABK and a desk.

14
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:00,520
Fantastic.

15
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:05,620
One word join across from the person to the person product to the product table.

16
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:11,920
Now you've got a complete answer, where from ABK you can see that here's the products that ABK has purchased,

17
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:15,620
this chair, lamp and a desk, and you can get the details about those purchases.

18
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:17,940
What if you extended that question?

19
00:01:18,280 --> 00:01:21,420
And you asked, who else bought products that ABK bought?

20
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:25,500
Well, you'd start with the same joins, going from ABK to the products,

21
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,140
and then you would join back to the person product table,

22
00:01:28,460 --> 00:01:30,660
and from there, back to the person table,

23
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:36,660
and now you would get this person EE who has bought some products that ABK also bought.

24
00:01:37,040 --> 00:01:40,400
But notice that EE has also bought one extra product.

25
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:42,140
Hmm, that's interesting.

26
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:46,580
So we could probably extend this one more step and ask,

27
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,460
well, what product should we recommend that ABK buy next?

28
00:01:51,020 --> 00:01:53,800
Now this is kind of the basics of doing a recommendation query.

29
00:01:54,320 --> 00:02:00,200
If you've got some purchase pattern from ABK that matches a purchase pattern from some other person

30
00:02:00,200 --> 00:02:05,680
or other group of people, the thing you want to recommend to ABK is what that other group of people bought

31
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:07,020
that ABK has not.

32
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:10,880
So the same joins apply from ABK through to the products,

33
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:13,620
back to the other people that have bought those products.

34
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:15,220
There's too many joins.

35
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:16,920
It's getting a little confusing.

36
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:20,760
There's actually a nicer way to actually think about this and to query this.

37
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:22,540
Let's turn this into a graph.

38
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:26,640
The first step is to just drop all the records that don't matter,

39
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:28,960
all those gray records that weren't part of the original query.

40
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:32,520
Let's put those aside for the moment and focus on the records that we know about

41
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:35,840
that are part of the data set so far that matters for this query,

42
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,980
about recommending something to ABK.

43
00:02:38,660 --> 00:02:40,800
Get rid of all the join table records in the middle,

44
00:02:41,040 --> 00:02:45,420
turn those into arrows connecting ABK directly over to the products that he has purchased,

45
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:48,680
and the same thing for EE over to the products that they have purchased.

46
00:02:49,160 --> 00:02:51,580
And now just rearrange the records a little bit.

47
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:54,700
You've got ABK on one side, EE on the other side,

48
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:56,780
and now it looks much nicer.

49
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:58,540
It's much easier to understand what's going on.

50
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:01,680
Both ABK and EE are connected to these products in the middle,

51
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:07,760
but then also this person EE is connected to another product that has no connection to ABK.

52
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:12,620
You can start to turn this into a query by rephrasing it as a pattern match.

53
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:15,000
Starting with ABK on the left side,

54
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:18,820
you can describe a query in a query language called Cypher

55
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:23,380
where it looks a little bit like SQL if SQL had pattern matching abilities.

56
00:03:23,980 --> 00:03:28,720
So here we're going to describe a pattern that describes the data records that you want to find.

57
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:34,160
We're going to match from the data record that we're going to call ABK that has a label of person,

58
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:38,660
and that has a property with the name key where the value of it is ABK.

59
00:03:39,020 --> 00:03:42,320
So that's in round parentheses. We know that that's a node in graph terms.

60
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:47,880
And then we kind of have this right-facing arrow with colon purchased in there.

61
00:03:48,040 --> 00:03:50,360
That's a relationship type that is the purchased.

62
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:57,320
So match a pattern where ABK purchased over to some other parentheses that we're going to call ABK products.

63
00:03:57,320 --> 00:04:01,600
That's going to be a pattern match from ABK to the products that ABK matched.

64
00:04:01,800 --> 00:04:05,600
We can return those directly, and that's going to be the products in the middle there.

65
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:11,200
We can extend that pattern to include the other people who bought those products.

66
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:14,180
Since we went from ABK to the products that ABK purchased,

67
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:18,420
we can also have relationships going in to those products and say,

68
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:20,620
okay, there's some other people who bought those products.

69
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,300
This pattern will find all of the products that people have in common with ABK.

70
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:38,560
We can extend it a little bit more to ask that recommendation question problem, where instead of saying we just want to find these other products, the other products have a unique property where they're actually something that ABK does not have.

71
00:04:38,820 --> 00:04:45,740
So we have the same pattern match from ABK to some products to some other people, and then extend that to other products.

72
00:04:46,020 --> 00:04:53,700
And now we include a predicate saying we actually want a negative pattern to be true, that ABK has not purchased those other products.

73
00:04:53,700 --> 00:05:02,460
So where not ABK purchased those other products? So down there on the bottom, that dotted line from ABK to the couch, we want that to not exist.

74
00:05:02,880 --> 00:05:07,920
Now we're describing a pattern that both has records that should exist, something that should not exist.

75
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:12,880
The other products now will be reduced to only products that ABK has not purchased.

76
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:15,500
That's what we're going to use as a recommendation that he buy.

77
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:18,440
So what really is a knowledge graph?

78
00:05:18,960 --> 00:05:26,920
It's a kind of database that represents information as nodes, representing things like people, products, blogs, whatever you might want to have as records in a database.

79
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:34,000
But then also relationships that aren't simply a convention of using a join table, but that are first class citizen.

80
00:05:34,180 --> 00:05:37,700
They're actually data records inside of the database that have a semantic meaning.

81
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:42,600
They actually represent how two nodes are connected, and they add information about those two nodes.

82
00:05:43,020 --> 00:05:50,920
So both the nodes and relationships have key value properties, they have multiple labels on nodes, relationships always have a direction, and they have a single type.

83
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:54,840
And the query language that you use for doing pattern matching is called Cypher.

84
00:05:55,260 --> 00:06:01,840
And it turns out that this is super nice for mapping to natural language, and really convenient for working with GenAI and LLMs,

85
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:03,720
because LLMs are really good at natural language.

86
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:10,920
If we go back for a moment here, and just look at that query at the top, you can read that out loud and make sense of it.

87
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:17,520
Match ABK, who's a person whose name is ABK, that bought some products that some other people have bought.

88
00:06:17,820 --> 00:06:21,800
They've bought some other products, where ABK has not purchased those other products.

89
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:29,060
The other thing that's interesting about knowledge graphs is that they're really convened for combining unstructured data along with that structured data,

90
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:34,200
whether that was some products that we have or people, whatever it might be in structured data.

91
00:06:34,460 --> 00:06:39,640
You can then add any kind of chunked text, do a vector representation of that, store that in the database as well,

92
00:06:39,640 --> 00:06:45,620
and combine both vector similarity search and pattern matching together for all kinds of powerful access patterns.

93
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:52,020
For example, if you want to do root cause analysis, and you imagine that you're a furniture manufacturer

94
00:06:52,560 --> 00:06:55,200
looking to understand customer complaints about your products,

95
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:59,560
so you turn to your team of experts, data engineering, data analysis team, and say,

96
00:06:59,660 --> 00:07:01,840
Hey, can you do some root cause analysis?

97
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,280
I've heard a lot of complaints out on the internet about our products.

98
00:07:05,280 --> 00:07:07,140
Let's try to figure out what's going on.

99
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:10,040
So the root cause analysis would include questions like,

100
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:11,480
which products have the most issues?

101
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:13,520
And then, based on those products,

102
00:07:13,660 --> 00:07:15,540
well, what part of the product is the actual problem?

103
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:17,640
And then you might want to ask a question like,

104
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:19,140
is there a problem with the part itself?

105
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:22,400
Perhaps there are other things that are going on, people just don't like the product?

106
00:07:22,860 --> 00:07:24,620
But if you can take corrective measures,

107
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:27,220
it would be because there's something either in the manufacturing process.

108
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:30,440
What you're looking for here is for corrective measures that you can take.

109
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:33,100
Do root cause analysis for something you can improve.

110
00:07:33,580 --> 00:07:36,540
So if it's a design problem, you could turn that over to the design team.

111
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:38,720
They could take a look at what people's complaints are about.

112
00:07:39,240 --> 00:07:42,640
But if it's a manufacturing problem or a problem with a part somewhere,

113
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:45,840
you can do some analysis on that, find the part problem,

114
00:07:46,040 --> 00:07:47,940
and either swap out the part for a new one,

115
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:50,100
or figure out how to improve the process itself.

116
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:53,480
So as a data engineer, if you were handed this task,

117
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:54,960
you would start with the usual thing.

118
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:56,400
Ask some clarifying questions.

119
00:07:57,280 --> 00:07:58,640
Why are we really doing this?

120
00:07:58,640 --> 00:08:02,920
You want to be able to understand that there's possible manufacturing problems in the supply chain.

121
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:03,960
Cool. Okay.

122
00:08:04,680 --> 00:08:07,280
Second question is, or if that's what we want to find out,

123
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:10,080
what data is available that we can do this analysis with?

124
00:08:10,800 --> 00:08:13,480
So it turns out in this scenario, we've got a bill of materials,

125
00:08:13,980 --> 00:08:17,980
some CSV files that connect the product all the way to the suppliers from those products.

126
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:20,000
Maybe it came from some spreadsheets.

127
00:08:20,120 --> 00:08:23,900
Maybe it came from some relational database that isn't really suited for doing analysis.

128
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:25,180
That's okay, too.

129
00:08:25,860 --> 00:08:29,940
And you also have some data from user reviews that you script from some websites on the internet.

130
00:08:30,480 --> 00:08:34,380
You want to be able to combine those into one dataset that you can then do some analysis on.

131
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:38,560
So to do that analysis, you're going to build a knowledge graph that connects all the data.

132
00:08:39,180 --> 00:08:41,620
So here's what the target data model is going to look like.

133
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:44,800
On the left, in the sort of beige boxes over there,

134
00:08:44,880 --> 00:08:46,660
you see the CSV files that are available.

135
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:51,400
Each one of those is going to turn into nodes in the graph or relationships in the graph.

136
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:54,880
That's going to be the kind of what we're going to call the domain graph.

137
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:57,460
On the right end side, on the bottom there,

138
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:00,780
you also see some beige boxes that represent markdown files.

139
00:09:01,020 --> 00:09:03,020
Those markdown files are going to be chunked up.

140
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:05,500
They're going to create some documents inside of the graph as well.

141
00:09:05,760 --> 00:09:07,580
And then all of this is going to get connected.

142
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:11,840
We'll look at some of the details as we actually go through doing the agent descriptions themselves.

143
00:09:12,420 --> 00:09:17,380
But what I want to call out here for you right now is that these are really very connected,

144
00:09:17,380 --> 00:09:19,380
but also very distinct parts of the graph.

145
00:09:19,380 --> 00:09:23,420
We've got the data sources, the structured data on the one side there.

146
00:09:23,740 --> 00:09:26,200
That's going to turn into what we call the domain graph.

147
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:30,080
So that's the structured data that we know that we can query with pattern matching.

148
00:09:30,660 --> 00:09:34,860
On the other side, the unstructured data sources are not going to be queried in the same way,

149
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:37,240
and they're not going to be imported in the same way either.

150
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:40,420
So they're going to turn into what we call the lexical graph,

151
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:42,600
which represents the original textual data,

152
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:45,100
plus also the structure around that textual data.

153
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:49,400
To connect those two pieces, the unstructured data and the structured data,

154
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:52,920
We have got this part in the middle,叫 us the subject graph.

155
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:57,420
The subject graph is going to be subjects, or entities,それでは物件取得到的重per состав简单。

156
00:09:57,840 --> 00:10:04,100
Matheme notes,材分扇出印象,一项是 бок材,对于用途容易以筹签局,

157
00:10:04,220 --> 00:10:06,600
We're going to find those, call those things subjects,

158
00:10:07,060 --> 00:10:10,680
And these subjects will be connected to objects, so they talk about something.

159
00:10:12,020 --> 00:10:14,320
Let's say that It's Me, some user BPK,

160
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:22,820
ABK loves this table. And so there will be a Subject Predicate object in the subject graph that talks about ABK loving some table.

161
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:30,000
That also, because ABK loves a table, the table can correspond with the product that we have in the structured data.

162
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:34,820
If ABK mentions a table and the table is a product that exists in the structured data,

163
00:10:35,060 --> 00:10:43,040
we can then connect the entities that we've extracted from the text all the way over to the structured data that we've got in from the CSV files.

